window.PW_CONTENT = window.PW_CONTENT || {};
window.PW_CONTENT.en = (window.PW_CONTENT.en || []).concat([
/* ---------- ART ---------- */
{id:"art_perspective",cat:"art",t:"Perspective",
 s:"A way of drawing or painting that makes a flat picture look as if it has depth and distance.",
 l:"In linear perspective, parallel lines such as the edges of a road or a row of houses are drawn so that they meet at a point on the horizon, called the vanishing point. Objects are drawn smaller the further away they are meant to be. Artists also use aerial perspective: distant hills are painted paler, bluer and less sharp, the way the air makes them look in real life. Perspective is a set of drawing rules, not a style, and many artists deliberately break it.",
 x:"In her drawing of the street, all the rooftops lead to one vanishing point in the middle.",
 f:"The Florentine architect Filippo Brunelleschi is credited with demonstrating linear perspective in the early fifteenth century, and Leon Battista Alberti explained it in writing in his book On Painting in fourteen thirty-five.",
 js:"A drawing trick that makes things look near or far away.",
 jl:"When you look down a long road, its sides seem to come closer together far away, and the trees at the end look tiny. Artists copy this on paper: things that are far away are drawn smaller, and lines meet in one point. That is perspective.",
 jx:"Mia drew a train track that got narrower and narrower until it met in one dot."},
{id:"art_chiaroscuro",cat:"art",t:"Chiaroscuro",
 s:"The use of strong contrast between light and dark in a picture to model shapes and create drama.",
 l:"With chiaroscuro, an artist lets light fall strongly on some parts of a scene while other parts sink into deep shadow. This makes faces and bodies look rounded and solid, and it can make a painting feel tense, mysterious or theatrical. The Italian painter Caravaggio pushed the effect so far that his figures seem to step out of darkness, and Rembrandt used it to make faces glow. The idea is also used in drawing, photography and film lighting.",
 x:"The candle in the painting lights only the children's faces, a perfect example of chiaroscuro.",
 f:"The word is Italian and joins chiaro, meaning light or clear, with scuro, meaning dark.",
 js:"Using very bright light and very dark shadow in a picture.",
 jl:"Imagine shining a torch on a face in a dark room. One side is bright, the other is in shadow. Some painters loved this look and painted strong light next to deep dark. It makes their pictures look almost real, and a little bit magical.",
 jx:"We made chiaroscuro photos with a torch and a dark bedroom."},
{id:"art_fresco",cat:"art",t:"Fresco",
 s:"A wall painting made with pigment on fresh, wet lime plaster, so that the colour becomes part of the wall.",
 l:"In true fresco, the painter mixes pigments with water and brushes them onto plaster that is still damp. As the plaster dries, a chemical reaction binds the colour into the surface, which makes frescoes very long-lasting. Because the plaster dries within hours, artists prepare only the area they can finish that day, and the joins between these daily patches can sometimes still be seen. Paint added later onto dry plaster is called secco and tends to flake off more easily.",
 x:"The old chapel is famous for its frescoes of saints and angels.",
 f:"Michelangelo painted the ceiling of the Sistine Chapel in Rome in fresco between fifteen oh eight and fifteen twelve.",
 js:"A painting made straight onto a wet wall.",
 jl:"Some painters painted right onto walls and ceilings. They spread wet plaster, like a smooth paste, and painted on it before it dried. When it dried, the colours became part of the wall, so these paintings can last for hundreds of years.",
 jx:"In Italy we saw a fresco on a church ceiling that was five hundred years old."},
{id:"art_primary_colours",cat:"art",t:"Primary colours",
 s:"A small set of colours that can be mixed to make many others but cannot be made by mixing.",
 l:"In traditional painting, the primary colours are red, yellow and blue, and mixing two of them gives the secondary colours orange, green and purple. Which colours count as primary depends on how colour is made, though. Screens make colour from light, and their primaries are red, green and blue; adding all three gives white. Printers mix inks, which absorb light, and use cyan, magenta and yellow. No set of three paints can actually produce every colour we can see.",
 x:"With just the three primary colours, the class mixed a whole rainbow of paints.",
 f:"Colour printers add black ink to cyan, magenta and yellow, which is why the system is called CMYK; the K stands for key, a printing term for the black plate.",
 js:"Colours you can mix to make lots of other colours.",
 jl:"In your paint box, red, yellow and blue are special. You cannot make them by mixing, but you can mix them to make new colours. Yellow and blue make green, red and yellow make orange, and red and blue make purple.",
 jx:"Zara mixed yellow and blue and shouted: I made green!"},
{id:"art_complementary",cat:"art",t:"Complementary colours",
 s:"Pairs of colours that sit opposite each other on the colour wheel, such as blue and orange.",
 l:"On a colour wheel, each colour has an opposite: red and green, blue and orange, yellow and purple. When complementary colours are placed side by side, each makes the other look brighter and stronger, which artists and designers use to make things stand out. When they are mixed together as paints, they dull each other towards brown or grey, which is handy for toning down a colour that is too loud. Many film posters rely on an orange and blue pairing.",
 x:"The orange boat really pops against the blue sea because they are complementary colours.",
 f:"Isaac Newton arranged the colours of the spectrum in a circle in his book Opticks, published in seventeen oh four, an early form of the colour wheel.",
 js:"Two colours that are opposites and make each other look brighter.",
 jl:"Some colours are like best friends who are total opposites, such as blue and orange, or red and green. When you put them next to each other, both look extra bright. That is why a red apple looks so shiny among green leaves.",
 jx:"I painted a purple butterfly on a yellow flower so it would really stand out."},
{id:"art_still_life",cat:"art",t:"Still life",
 s:"A picture of objects that do not move, such as fruit, flowers, bottles or books.",
 l:"A still life arranges ordinary things on a table or shelf and studies their shapes, colours, textures and the way light falls on them. Because the objects stay put, still lifes are a classic way to practise drawing and painting. In the seventeenth century, Dutch painters made still life a major art form, painting glistening fruit, glassware and flowers in astonishing detail. Some still lifes hide messages: a wilting flower or a burnt-out candle can remind viewers that time passes.",
 x:"For art homework, he drew a still life of a teapot, two lemons and a spoon.",
 f:"The English term comes from the Dutch word stilleven, while French and Polish use expressions meaning dead nature, nature morte and martwa natura.",
 js:"A picture of things that don't move, like fruit or flowers.",
 jl:"A still life is a picture of objects sitting quietly, such as apples in a bowl, a vase of flowers or a pair of shoes. Artists like them because the things keep still, so you can look as long as you need to.",
 jx:"Our class painted a still life of a pumpkin, three apples and a jug."},
{id:"art_self_portrait",cat:"art",t:"Self-portrait",
 s:"A portrait an artist makes of themselves.",
 l:"To make a self-portrait, artists traditionally looked in a mirror, so many self-portraits show the face reversed and the eyes looking straight out. Self-portraits let artists practise without paying a model, but they are also a way to show how they see themselves, their mood, their job or their place in the world. Some artists made dozens over a lifetime, so we can watch them age. A photo self-portrait taken at arm's length is today usually called a selfie.",
 x:"Frida Kahlo's self-portraits often show her surrounded by plants and animals.",
 f:"Albrecht Dürer drew a self-portrait in silverpoint when he was about thirteen, in fourteen eighty-four; it is one of the earliest surviving self-portraits by a European child.",
 js:"A picture an artist makes of their own face.",
 jl:"A self-portrait is a picture of yourself that you make yourself. Artists often looked in a mirror while they painted. You can show your face, your favourite clothes, or things you love, like your pet or your football.",
 jx:"For my self-portrait I drew myself holding my cat and wearing my yellow hat."},
{id:"art_impressionism",cat:"art",t:"Impressionism",
 s:"An art movement from nineteenth-century France that tried to capture light, colour and fleeting moments.",
 l:"Impressionist painters such as Claude Monet, Pierre-Auguste Renoir, Camille Pissarro and Berthe Morisot often worked outdoors and painted quickly with visible dabs of colour. They cared less about fine detail and more about how a scene looked at a certain moment: sunlight on water, mist at a station, shadows that are blue or purple rather than black. At first, many critics thought their pictures looked unfinished. Today Impressionism is among the most popular styles in museums.",
 x:"Up close the painting looks like messy dabs, but from across the room you see a sunny Impressionist garden.",
 f:"The name came from Monet's painting Impression, Sunrise; a critic used the word mockingly after the group's first exhibition in Paris in eighteen seventy-four.",
 js:"A way of painting with quick dabs of colour to show light and moments.",
 jl:"Some painters in France went outside and painted very fast, before the sun moved and the light changed. Their brushstrokes look like little dabs. Up close it looks messy, but step back and you see sparkling water, flowers or clouds.",
 jx:"We painted the school garden like Impressionists, quickly, before the clouds came."},
{id:"art_cubism",cat:"art",t:"Cubism",
 s:"An early twentieth-century art style that breaks objects into geometric shapes and shows several viewpoints at once.",
 l:"Cubism was developed in Paris by Pablo Picasso and Georges Braque from around nineteen oh seven. Instead of painting a guitar or a face from one fixed position, they took it apart into flat planes and angles and showed different sides together, as if you were walking around it. Colours were often muted at first, so that attention went to structure. Cubism changed how artists thought about space and opened the way to abstract art, collage and much of modern design.",
 x:"In the Cubist portrait you can see the woman's nose from the side and both eyes from the front.",
 f:"Picasso's large painting Les Demoiselles d'Avignon, finished in nineteen oh seven, is often seen as a forerunner of Cubism.",
 js:"A style of art where things are shown as blocks and shapes, from many sides at once.",
 jl:"Cubist artists asked: why paint something from just one side? So they showed the front and the side at the same time, using lots of angles and shapes. A face might have a nose sideways and two eyes looking at you.",
 jx:"Oskar made a Cubist dog with square ears and a triangle nose."},
{id:"art_surrealism",cat:"art",t:"Surrealism",
 s:"A twentieth-century art and literary movement that explored dreams, the unconscious and strange, impossible combinations.",
 l:"Surrealism began in Paris in the nineteen twenties. Its artists wanted to free the imagination from logic and show the world of dreams. Some, like Salvador Dalí and René Magritte, painted impossible scenes in a very realistic style, such as melting clocks or a pipe labelled 'this is not a pipe'. Others, like Joan Miró, used playful, almost abstract shapes. In everyday speech, surreal means oddly dreamlike, as in a surreal day when nothing made sense.",
 x:"The painting of a train coming out of a fireplace is a famous Surrealist image by Magritte.",
 f:"The poet André Breton published the first Manifesto of Surrealism in nineteen twenty-four.",
 js:"Art that looks like a strange dream.",
 jl:"Surrealist artists painted things you could only see in dreams: a clock melting like cheese, a fish with legs, an apple floating in front of a face. They wanted to show that imagination can be wild and surprising.",
 jx:"For a Surrealist picture, Lena drew a giraffe wearing roller skates on the Moon."},
{id:"art_abstract",cat:"art",t:"Abstract art",
 s:"Art that does not try to show real objects but uses shapes, colours, lines and textures for their own sake.",
 l:"An abstract painting may be made only of coloured squares, splashes, stripes or flowing forms. Some abstract art starts from something real and simplifies it until it is unrecognisable; other works never referred to anything visible at all. Artists use it to express feelings, rhythm or ideas, and viewers are free to bring their own response. Abstract does not mean random: many abstract works are carefully planned, with balance and colour chosen as deliberately as in any portrait.",
 x:"The abstract painting is just three red stripes, but it somehow feels calm.",
 f:"Wassily Kandinsky is often called a founder of abstract art, but the Swedish painter Hilma af Klint was already making abstract paintings from nineteen oh six, and they were shown widely only decades after her death.",
 js:"Art made of shapes and colours instead of real things.",
 jl:"Abstract art doesn't show a house, a dog or a person. It shows colours, lines and shapes, like swirls, dots or stripes. The fun part is that everyone can feel something different when they look at it.",
 jx:"My abstract picture is all blue swirls, because that's how the sea makes me feel."},
{id:"art_mosaic",cat:"art",t:"Mosaic",
 s:"A picture or pattern made from many small pieces of stone, glass or tile set in cement or plaster.",
 l:"Mosaic makers cut or collect small pieces, called tesserae, and fix them close together on a wall, floor or ceiling. Seen from a distance, the pieces merge into a picture. Ancient Romans decorated floors with mosaics of animals, gods and everyday scenes, and Byzantine artists covered church walls with glittering gold glass mosaics, such as those in Ravenna in Italy. Mosaic is very durable, so many have survived for well over a thousand years.",
 x:"The swimming pool has a mosaic of a dolphin made of tiny blue tiles.",
 f:"The small pieces of a mosaic are called tesserae, from a Latin word linked to the Greek word for four, because they are often roughly square with four corners.",
 js:"A picture made of lots of tiny coloured pieces.",
 jl:"A mosaic is like a puzzle that you glue together. You take tiny bits of coloured stone, glass or tile and put them side by side until they make a picture. The Romans made mosaics on their floors.",
 jx:"We made a mosaic of a sun using pieces of coloured paper."},
{id:"art_watercolour",cat:"art",t:"Watercolour",
 s:"Paint made of pigment and a water-soluble binder, used in thin, transparent layers, usually on paper.",
 l:"Watercolour is applied with plenty of water, so the colours are transparent and the white of the paper shines through. Because of this, painters usually keep white areas by leaving the paper bare instead of adding white paint, and they work from light to dark. Wet colours can flow into each other in soft blooms, which makes watercolour lively but hard to control. It dries fast, is easy to carry and is popular for sketches outdoors, botanical studies and book illustrations.",
 x:"She painted the misty lake in watercolour, letting the greys run into each other.",
 f:"Albrecht Dürer's Young Hare from fifteen oh two, painted mostly in watercolour and gouache, is one of the most famous animal pictures in European art.",
 js:"Paint you mix with water that looks light and see-through.",
 jl:"Watercolour paints come in little blocks or tubes. You wet your brush, and the colours become light and see-through, so the white paper shows. If you paint wet colours next to each other, they run and mix in pretty ways.",
 jx:"I used watercolours to paint a sunset, and the orange ran into the pink."},
{id:"art_oil_paint",cat:"art",t:"Oil paint",
 s:"Paint made by mixing pigment with a drying oil, usually linseed oil.",
 l:"Oil paint dries slowly, over days rather than minutes, so artists can blend colours smoothly on the canvas and change their minds. It can be used in thin, glowing, transparent layers called glazes, or thickly so that the brushstrokes stand out, a technique called impasto. Painters traditionally work fat over lean, adding more oil in upper layers so the painting does not crack. Oil paint became the main medium of European painting from the fifteenth century onwards.",
 x:"Van Gogh laid on oil paint so thickly that you can see every brushstroke.",
 f:"Jan van Eyck is often wrongly called the inventor of oil painting; oils were used earlier, but he and other Flemish painters of the fifteenth century brought the technique to astonishing perfection.",
 js:"Thick, shiny paint that dries very slowly.",
 jl:"Many famous paintings in museums are made with oil paint. It is thick and shiny and takes days to dry. That means artists can keep mixing and changing the picture for a long time.",
 jx:"At the museum, we could see bumpy oil paint on the sunflower picture."},
{id:"art_easel",cat:"art",t:"Easel",
 s:"A stand that holds a painting or drawing board upright while the artist works.",
 l:"An easel holds the canvas or board at a comfortable height and angle, so the artist can stand or sit back and see the whole picture without distortion. Studio easels are heavy and steady, while folding field easels are light enough to carry outdoors. Small tabletop easels are used for displaying pictures or for working at a desk. Teachers and presenters also use easels to hold flip charts and signs.",
 x:"He set up his easel on the beach and started painting the waves.",
 f:"The English word easel comes from the Dutch ezel, meaning donkey, because the stand carries a load the way a donkey does.",
 js:"A stand that holds a picture while you paint it.",
 jl:"An easel is a stand with legs that holds your paper or canvas up, so you don't have to paint flat on a table. Artists take folding easels outside to paint trees, hills and the sea.",
 jx:"In the classroom there are three easels for painting."},
{id:"art_composition",cat:"art",t:"Composition",
 s:"The way the parts of a picture are arranged to make a balanced, interesting whole.",
 l:"Composition is about where things are placed, how big they are, how they overlap and where the viewer's eye travels. Artists use lines that lead the eye, groups of shapes, contrasts of light and colour, and empty areas to guide attention to what matters most. A well-known guideline is the rule of thirds, which places important elements along imaginary lines dividing the picture into thirds rather than right in the centre. Good composition can make even a simple subject striking.",
 x:"The composition works because the path in the photo leads your eye straight to the lighthouse.",
 f:"Many phone cameras can show a grid of nine equal rectangles on the screen, which helps photographers use the rule of thirds.",
 js:"How things are arranged in a picture.",
 jl:"Composition means deciding where everything goes in your picture. Is the tree in the middle or on the side? Is the cat big or small? Moving things around can make the same picture look boring or amazing.",
 jx:"I moved the boat to the side of my picture, and the composition looked much better."},
{id:"art_contrapposto",cat:"art",t:"Contrapposto",
 s:"A relaxed standing pose in sculpture and painting, with the weight on one leg so that the hips and shoulders tilt in opposite directions.",
 l:"In contrapposto, a figure rests its weight on one leg while the other knee is bent. This makes the hips tilt one way and the shoulders the other, giving the body a gentle S-shaped curve. The pose looks natural and alive, in contrast to stiff figures standing straight with both feet planted. Ancient Greek sculptors developed it in the fifth century before the common era, and Renaissance artists revived it. You can see the same pose in people waiting at a bus stop.",
 x:"The statue looks relaxed because of its contrapposto: all the weight is on the right leg.",
 f:"Michelangelo's marble David, carved between fifteen oh one and fifteen oh four, is one of the best-known examples of contrapposto.",
 js:"A relaxed way of standing, with most of your weight on one leg.",
 jl:"Stand up and lean on one leg, letting the other knee bend a little. Your hips and shoulders tilt. Ancient Greek sculptors noticed that this makes a statue look relaxed and alive, not stiff like a soldier.",
 jx:"Our teacher showed us contrapposto, and the whole class stood like statues."},
{id:"art_sfumato",cat:"art",t:"Sfumato",
 s:"A painting technique of very soft, gradual transitions between colours and tones, without sharp outlines.",
 l:"Sfumato blends light into shadow so gently that you cannot see where one ends and the other begins, giving a soft, hazy look. Painters achieved it with many very thin, translucent layers of oil paint. Leonardo da Vinci is the artist most associated with sfumato; it gives the faces in his paintings their mysterious, lifelike softness, especially around the eyes and the corners of the mouth. It is quite different from chiaroscuro, which stresses strong contrast.",
 x:"The sfumato around her smile makes it hard to tell whether she is smiling at all.",
 f:"The word is Italian and comes from fumo, meaning smoke, so it suggests shapes melting softly as if seen through smoke.",
 js:"A way of painting so softly that there are no hard lines.",
 jl:"Sfumato is when an artist blends colours so softly that they seem to melt into each other, like a cloud. Leonardo da Vinci painted the Mona Lisa this way. That's why her face looks so gentle.",
 jx:"I rubbed my pencil shading with my finger to make a sfumato sky."},
{id:"art_trompe_loeil",cat:"art",t:"Trompe-l'oeil",
 s:"Art that tricks the eye into seeing painted things as real, three-dimensional objects.",
 l:"Trompe-l'oeil artists use perspective, light and shadow so cleverly that a flat wall seems to have a window, a staircase or a shelf of books. Painters have used it for centuries, for example to paint fake domes, columns and open skies on ceilings. Today it appears in street art, such as chalk drawings of holes in the pavement, and in murals that make plain buildings look decorated. The effect usually works best from one particular viewpoint.",
 x:"The trompe-l'oeil mural made the wall look like an open door to a garden.",
 f:"In the church of Sant'Ignazio in Rome, Andrea Pozzo painted a dome on a flat ceiling in the late seventeenth century, and from the right spot on the floor it looks real.",
 js:"A painting that tricks your eyes into thinking it is real.",
 jl:"Some painters are so clever that their pictures fool you. A painted window on a wall looks like you could open it, or a chalk drawing on the ground looks like a deep hole. That trick is called trompe-l'oeil.",
 jx:"I almost stepped around the chalk hole on the pavement, but it was trompe-l'oeil."},
{id:"art_pointillism",cat:"art",t:"Pointillism",
 s:"A painting technique that uses small dots of pure colour which blend in the viewer's eye.",
 l:"Instead of mixing paints on the palette, pointillist painters put separate dots of unmixed colour side by side. From a distance, the eye combines them, so blue and yellow dots placed close together can read as a shimmering green. The technique was developed in the eighteen eighties by Georges Seurat and Paul Signac, who were interested in the science of colour and perception. Screens and printed pictures also build images out of tiny dots, which is a similar idea.",
 x:"Look closely at the pointillist painting and the sky turns into thousands of dots.",
 f:"Georges Seurat worked for about two years, from eighteen eighty-four to eighteen eighty-six, on his huge dotted painting A Sunday Afternoon on the Island of La Grande Jatte.",
 js:"Painting a picture with lots of tiny dots.",
 jl:"Pointillism means painting with dots instead of brushstrokes. Up close you see only dots of colour. Step back, and the dots join together into trees, people and water. You can try it with a cotton bud dipped in paint.",
 jx:"We made pointillism pictures of a flower using cotton buds and paint."},
{id:"art_icon",cat:"art",t:"Icon",
 s:"A sacred image of Christ, Mary or a saint, painted on a wooden panel, especially in Eastern Christian traditions.",
 l:"Icons have a special role in Orthodox and Eastern Catholic churches, where they are treated as windows to the holy rather than ordinary decoration. They follow traditional rules: flat, golden backgrounds, calm faces, symbolic colours and a perspective that seems to come towards the viewer. Icons are traditionally made with egg tempera on a prepared wooden board. In computing and everyday life, the word icon now also means a small symbol on a screen or a person widely admired.",
 x:"In the Orthodox church, the wall of icons in front of the altar shimmered with gold.",
 f:"In Orthodox tradition, icons are often said to be written rather than painted, because the Greek verb graphein can mean both to write and to draw.",
 js:"A holy picture painted on wood, often with gold.",
 jl:"An icon is a special religious picture, often of Mary or a saint, painted on a piece of wood with a shiny gold background. In some churches people light candles in front of them. The small pictures on a tablet screen are called icons too.",
 jx:"Grandma has a small icon with a golden background in her living room."},
{id:"art_collage",cat:"art",t:"Collage",
 s:"Artwork made by sticking pieces of paper, photographs, fabric or other materials onto a surface.",
 l:"A collage combines different materials, such as magazine cuttings, tickets, wallpaper, newspaper, string or leaves, into one composition. Unexpected combinations can be funny, surprising or political. Collage became an important modern art technique in the early twentieth century and is still used in posters, album covers and digital design, where images are layered on screen. It is popular in schools too, because anyone can do it with scissors and glue.",
 x:"Her collage mixes old maps, bus tickets and photos from the family holiday.",
 f:"The word comes from the French coller, meaning to glue, and Georges Braque and Pablo Picasso began gluing paper into their pictures around nineteen twelve.",
 js:"A picture made by gluing bits of paper and other things together.",
 jl:"To make a collage, you cut out pieces of coloured paper, pictures from magazines, fabric or even leaves, and glue them onto a sheet. Together they make a new picture. You can make anything, from a monster to a city.",
 jx:"Ben made a collage of a robot from magazine pictures and bottle tops."},
{id:"art_graffiti",cat:"art",t:"Graffiti",
 s:"Writing or drawings on walls and other public surfaces, from scratched words to large spray-painted pieces.",
 l:"Graffiti ranges from simple tags, which are stylised signatures, to elaborate colourful murals. Modern graffiti grew in New York in the nineteen seventies, especially on subway trains, and spread around the world together with hip hop culture. Painting on property without permission is generally against the law, but many cities now offer legal walls and commission street artists. People still argue about where graffiti ends and street art begins.",
 x:"The old tunnel under the railway is covered in bright graffiti.",
 f:"The word comes from the Italian graffiare, to scratch, and ancient graffiti, including jokes, greetings and election slogans, survive on the walls of Pompeii.",
 js:"Words or pictures painted or scratched on walls.",
 jl:"Graffiti are words and pictures on walls, made with spray paint, pens or even scratches. Some are big and beautiful, made with permission. Others are painted where it isn't allowed, which can upset the owners of the wall.",
 jx:"The town let artists paint graffiti of animals on the wall by the skate park."},
{id:"art_printmaking",cat:"art",t:"Printmaking",
 s:"Making art by transferring ink from a prepared surface, such as a woodblock or metal plate, onto paper.",
 l:"In printmaking, the artist prepares a matrix and then prints from it, often many times. In a woodcut or linocut, parts of the block are cut away and ink sits on the raised surface. In etching and engraving, lines are cut or bitten with acid into a metal plate, and ink is pushed into these grooves. Screen printing pushes ink through a fine mesh. Each print from the same plate is an original, and artists often number them, for example five of fifty.",
 x:"In the workshop we carved a hedgehog into lino and printed it on cards.",
 f:"Albrecht Dürer made his famous woodcut of a rhinoceros in fifteen fifteen without ever seeing the animal, working from a written description and a sketch sent from Lisbon.",
 js:"Making pictures by pressing an inked shape onto paper.",
 jl:"Printmaking is like a very fancy stamp. You carve a picture into a block of wood or soft lino, roll ink onto it, and press paper on top. You can make the same picture again and again.",
 jx:"We cut a potato into a star and printed stars all over the wrapping paper."},
{id:"art_restoration",cat:"art",t:"Art restoration",
 s:"The careful work of cleaning, repairing and preserving damaged works of art.",
 l:"Restorers, also called conservators, study an artwork's materials and condition before touching it. They may remove dirt and yellowed varnish, secure flaking paint, repair tears or fill small losses. Modern practice stresses that changes should be minimal, well documented and, where possible, reversible, so later experts can undo them with better methods. Scientists help with X-rays, infrared imaging and paint analysis, which sometimes reveal earlier sketches or changes under the surface.",
 x:"After the art restoration, the bright blue of the sky in the painting appeared again.",
 f:"The great cleaning of Michelangelo's Sistine Chapel frescoes lasted from nineteen eighty to nineteen ninety-four and revealed far brighter colours than people had been used to.",
 js:"Carefully cleaning and fixing old works of art.",
 jl:"Very old paintings can get dirty, cracked or faded. Special experts clean them with tiny brushes and gentle liquids, and carefully fix the damage. They try never to change what the artist made.",
 jx:"At the museum, we watched a restorer cleaning a painting with a cotton swab."},
{id:"art_negative_space",cat:"art",t:"Negative space",
 s:"The empty space around and between the main subjects of a picture or sculpture.",
 l:"Negative space is everything that is not the subject: the sky around a tree, the gap between a cup and its handle. Artists pay attention to it because those empty shapes are just as important to the composition as the objects. Drawing the negative spaces instead of the object itself is a classic exercise that helps people draw more accurately. Designers use it cleverly in logos, where an empty area can form a second hidden picture.",
 x:"Look at the negative space between the two faces in the logo and you will see a vase.",
 f:"The famous picture that can be seen either as a vase or as two faces is called Rubin's vase, after the Danish psychologist Edgar Rubin, who wrote about it around nineteen fifteen.",
 js:"The empty space around the things in a picture.",
 jl:"When you draw a chair, there are empty spaces between its legs. Those empty shapes are called negative space. Artists look at them carefully, because they help the picture look right.",
 jx:"Instead of drawing the plant, we coloured in the negative space around its leaves."},
{id:"art_en_turner_prize",cat:"art",only:1,c:"United Kingdom",t:"Turner Prize",
 s:"A well-known annual award for contemporary art made by an artist born or working in Britain.",
 l:"The Turner Prize was founded in nineteen eighty-four and is organised by Tate. Each year a jury picks a shortlist of artists, their work is shown in an exhibition, and one winner is announced in a televised ceremony. The prize often goes to installations, video, sound or performance rather than traditional painting, so it regularly sparks debate in the British press about what counts as art. The exhibition is held at Tate Britain in London in some years and in other UK cities in others.",
 x:"Every year the Turner Prize shortlist gets people arguing about modern art.",
 f:"The prize is named after the painter J. M. W. Turner, whose portrait appears on the Bank of England's twenty-pound note introduced in twenty twenty.",
 js:"A famous British prize for modern art.",
 jl:"Every year in Britain, a group of experts chooses some artists making new and unusual art, and one of them wins the Turner Prize. Sometimes the art is a painting, but it can also be a film, a room full of objects or sounds.",
 jx:"My sister took me to see the Turner Prize exhibition, and one artwork was a room full of balloons."},
{id:"art_en_pre_raphaelites",cat:"art",only:1,c:"United Kingdom",t:"Pre-Raphaelites",
 s:"A group of Victorian English artists who painted in bright colours and sharp detail, inspired by art before Raphael.",
 l:"The Pre-Raphaelite Brotherhood was founded in London in eighteen forty-eight by young painters including Dante Gabriel Rossetti, John Everett Millais and William Holman Hunt. They rejected what they saw as the dull, formula-based teaching of the Royal Academy and looked back to medieval and early Renaissance art. Their paintings are full of jewel-like colour, precise plants and flowers, and subjects from legends, poetry and the Bible. Many of their works hang in Tate Britain and in galleries in Birmingham and Manchester.",
 x:"The Pre-Raphaelites painted every leaf and petal along the riverbank in astonishing detail.",
 f:"For Millais's painting Ophelia, the model Elizabeth Siddal posed for long sessions lying in a bath of water in his studio.",
 js:"A group of English painters who used very bright colours and tiny details.",
 jl:"In Victorian times, a group of young English painters wanted to paint in a fresh new way. They used bright, jewel-like colours and painted every flower and leaf carefully. Many of their pictures tell stories from old legends, like King Arthur.",
 jx:"At the gallery I counted the flowers in a Pre-Raphaelite painting and gave up at fifty."},
{id:"art_en_arts_and_crafts",cat:"art",only:1,c:"United Kingdom",t:"Arts and Crafts movement",
 s:"A British design movement of the late nineteenth century that valued handmade, beautiful, useful objects.",
 l:"The Arts and Crafts movement reacted against cheap, mass-produced goods of the industrial age. Its supporters, led by the designer, writer and campaigner William Morris, believed that everyday things such as furniture, wallpaper, textiles, books and pottery should be made with skill and care and be a pleasure to use. They admired medieval craft workshops and natural forms like leaves, birds and flowers. The movement influenced design across Europe and America.",
 x:"The house still has its original Arts and Crafts wallpaper with twisting willow leaves.",
 f:"In an eighteen eighty lecture, William Morris gave his famous advice: have nothing in your houses that you do not know to be useful, or believe to be beautiful.",
 js:"A group of designers who loved beautiful handmade things.",
 jl:"When factories began making lots of cheap things, some people in Britain felt sad that skilled handwork was disappearing. They made beautiful chairs, wallpaper and fabrics by hand, often with patterns of flowers and birds.",
 jx:"Our curtains have a flowery pattern designed by William Morris, from the Arts and Crafts movement."},
{id:"art_en_fourth_plinth",cat:"art",only:1,c:"United Kingdom",t:"Fourth Plinth",
 s:"A stone pedestal in London's Trafalgar Square that displays a changing series of specially commissioned contemporary artworks.",
 l:"Trafalgar Square has four large plinths at its corners. Three carry statues of historical figures, but the one in the north-west corner stood empty for more than a century and a half. Since the late nineteen nineties it has been used for temporary contemporary sculptures, and the Mayor of London's Fourth Plinth programme now chooses a new work every year or two. Past works have included a giant blue cockerel and a ship in a huge bottle.",
 x:"We went to Trafalgar Square to see the newest sculpture on the Fourth Plinth.",
 f:"The plinth was built in eighteen forty-one for a statue on horseback, but the money ran out, so it stayed empty for over a hundred and fifty years.",
 js:"A stand in a London square where a new statue appears every year or two.",
 jl:"In Trafalgar Square in London there are four big stone stands for statues. One stayed empty for a very long time because there was no money for a statue. Now artists take turns showing new, surprising sculptures on it.",
 jx:"On our trip to London, the Fourth Plinth had a giant thumbs-up sculpture on it."},
/* ---------- MEDICINE (+6) ---------- */
{id:"m_mri",cat:"med",t:"MRI scan",
 s:"A scan that uses a strong magnet and radio waves to make detailed pictures of the inside of the body.",
 l:"MRI stands for magnetic resonance imaging. The person lies on a table that slides into a tunnel-shaped machine, which creates a powerful magnetic field and sends radio-wave pulses. Hydrogen atoms in the body's tissues respond, and a computer turns the signals into detailed images, especially useful for the brain, spine, joints and soft tissues. The scan is painless but noisy, so patients get ear protection. Because of the magnet, metal objects and some implants must be checked first.",
 x:"The doctor ordered an MRI scan to look at the ligaments in her knee.",
 f:"MRI uses no X-rays or other ionising radiation; Paul Lauterbur and Peter Mansfield received the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine in two thousand and three for their work on it.",
 js:"A big machine that takes pictures of the inside of your body using a magnet.",
 jl:"An MRI machine is like a big tunnel with a strong magnet inside. You lie very still on a bed that slides into it. It makes loud knocking sounds, but it doesn't hurt. The doctor gets clear pictures of your insides, like your brain or knee.",
 jx:"Amir had an MRI scan and listened to music on headphones while the machine buzzed."},
{id:"m_x_ray",cat:"med",t:"X-ray",
 s:"A type of radiation that passes through soft tissue but is blocked by bone, used to make images of the inside of the body.",
 l:"In an X-ray image, dense structures such as bones and teeth absorb more radiation and appear white, while soft tissues and air appear in shades of grey and black. X-rays are quick and widely used to check for broken bones, chest infections or dental problems. The dose from a single ordinary X-ray is low, but staff still limit exposure, which is why they step behind a screen and why patients are asked if they might be pregnant. CT scanners use X-rays from many angles to build cross-section images.",
 x:"The X-ray showed a small crack in the bone of his wrist.",
 f:"Wilhelm Conrad Röntgen discovered X-rays in eighteen ninety-five, and one of his first X-ray images showed the bones of his wife's hand and her wedding ring.",
 js:"A special photo that shows your bones.",
 jl:"When someone might have broken a bone, a doctor takes an X-ray. It's a picture that shows the bones in white, because the special rays can pass through skin but not through bone. It takes just a moment and doesn't hurt.",
 jx:"After falling off my bike, the X-ray showed my arm was not broken."},
{id:"m_insulin",cat:"med",t:"Insulin",
 s:"A hormone made by the pancreas that helps the body's cells take in sugar from the blood.",
 l:"After we eat, sugar in the form of glucose enters the bloodstream, and the pancreas releases insulin, which acts like a key letting glucose into cells to be used for energy. In type one diabetes, the body makes little or no insulin, so people need to take it every day, by injection or through a small pump. In type two diabetes, the body does not respond to insulin well or does not make enough. Doctors and diabetes teams decide each person's treatment individually.",
 x:"Her insulin pump helps keep her blood sugar steady during football matches.",
 f:"Insulin was discovered in Toronto in nineteen twenty-one by Frederick Banting and Charles Best working with colleagues, and the first patient was treated with it in January nineteen twenty-two.",
 js:"Something your body makes that helps it use sugar for energy.",
 jl:"Your body turns food into a kind of sugar that gives you energy. Insulin is like a key that opens the doors of your body's cells so the sugar can get in. Some children have diabetes, so their bodies need help with insulin, often from a small pump or injections.",
 jx:"My friend Ola checks her blood sugar before PE and uses insulin when she needs it."},
{id:"m_microbiome",cat:"med",t:"Microbiome",
 s:"The huge community of bacteria, fungi and other microbes living in and on the human body.",
 l:"Trillions of microbes live on our skin, in our mouth and especially in our gut. Most are harmless or helpful: gut bacteria help break down fibre, make some vitamins and train the immune system. Diet, illness, medicines such as antibiotics and the environment all change the microbiome. Scientists are actively researching links between the microbiome and many conditions, but much is still unclear, so bold claims made in adverts for supplements should be treated with caution.",
 x:"Eating plenty of fibre from vegetables and beans feeds a healthy gut microbiome.",
 f:"It used to be claimed that bacteria in the body outnumber human cells ten to one, but a study published in twenty sixteen estimated the numbers are roughly equal.",
 js:"All the tiny living things, like helpful bacteria, that live in and on your body.",
 jl:"Your body is home to billions of tiny living things that are too small to see. Many live in your tummy and help you digest food. Most of them are friendly helpers, and eating fruit and vegetables keeps them happy.",
 jx:"We learned that the bacteria in our tummies help us digest our dinner."},
{id:"m_hand_hygiene",cat:"med",t:"Hand hygiene",
 s:"Cleaning hands with soap and water or an alcohol-based rub to remove germs and prevent infection.",
 l:"Hands touch our faces, food and other people all day, so they easily carry viruses and bacteria. Washing with soap and running water for about twenty seconds, rubbing palms, backs, between the fingers and under the nails, removes many germs. When soap is not available, alcohol-based hand rubs work against many germs, but not as well on very dirty hands. In hospitals, hand hygiene before and after touching each patient is one of the most effective ways to prevent infections.",
 x:"The nurse cleaned her hands before and after seeing every patient.",
 f:"In eighteen forty-seven in Vienna, the doctor Ignaz Semmelweis showed that when doctors washed their hands in a chlorine solution, infections among new mothers in his clinic fell sharply, but many colleagues rejected his idea.",
 js:"Washing your hands well to get rid of germs.",
 jl:"Germs are too tiny to see, but they get on our hands when we touch things. Washing with soap and water for as long as it takes to sing Happy Birthday twice gets rid of most of them. That helps stop you and others from getting ill.",
 jx:"Before lunch, everyone in our class washes their hands and sings the birthday song twice."},
{id:"m_en_prescription_charge",cat:"med",only:1,c:"England",t:"Prescription charge",
 s:"A fixed fee paid in England for each item on an NHS prescription, from which many people are exempt.",
 l:"In England, adults usually pay a set charge for each medicine or item on an NHS prescription, and the amount is reviewed regularly by the government. Many groups get prescriptions free, including children under sixteen, young people in full-time education up to eighteen, people aged sixty or over, pregnant people and new mothers with an exemption certificate, and people with certain medical conditions or on some benefits. People who need many items can buy a prepayment certificate to save money.",
 x:"He bought a prepayment certificate because he needs several items on prescription every month.",
 f:"Scotland, Wales and Northern Ireland have all abolished prescription charges, so they remain only in England.",
 js:"Money that some grown-ups in England pay for their medicine from the doctor.",
 jl:"When a doctor in England gives a grown-up a note for medicine, called a prescription, they often have to pay a small fee at the chemist. Children's medicines are free, and many other people don't have to pay either.",
 jx:"At the chemist, Dad paid for his prescription, but my medicine was free."},
/* ---------- LAW (+6) ---------- */
{id:"l_faulty_goods",cat:"law",c:"United Kingdom",t:"Consumer rights for faulty goods",
 s:"The legal rights a shopper has when something they buy is faulty, not as described or not fit for purpose.",
 l:"Under the Consumer Rights Act of twenty fifteen, goods bought from a trader must be of satisfactory quality, fit for purpose and as described. If they are not, the buyer has a short-term right to reject them and get a refund, usually within thirty days. After that, the trader normally gets one chance to repair or replace the item, and if that fails, the buyer can ask for a refund or a price reduction. These are legal rights, separate from a shop's own returns policy or any guarantee.",
 x:"The kettle broke after a week, so she used her consumer rights and got a full refund.",
 f:"Changing your mind is not covered: a shop does not have to take back an item that is not faulty, unless you bought it online or by phone, where a separate cancellation right applies.",
 js:"Your right to get things fixed or your money back if something you bought is broken.",
 jl:"If you buy a toy and it breaks straight away because it was badly made, that's not fair. The law says the shop must help, by fixing it, giving you a new one or giving your money back. Keep the receipt just in case.",
 jx:"My new headphones stopped working, so Mum took them back and the shop gave us new ones."},
{id:"l_notary",cat:"law",c:"England & Wales",t:"Notary",
 s:"A specially qualified lawyer who certifies documents and signatures, especially for use abroad.",
 l:"In England and Wales, notaries are a small branch of the legal profession. Most of their work is for documents that will be used in other countries: confirming a person's identity and signature, certifying copies, and preparing powers of attorney or company papers so that foreign authorities will accept them. Everyday legal matters at home, such as buying a house or making a will, are usually handled by solicitors instead. This is quite different from many European countries, where notaries play a central role in property deals.",
 x:"To sell her flat in Spain, she had to sign the papers in front of a notary in London.",
 f:"Notaries in England and Wales are appointed by the Faculty Office, which operates under the authority of the Archbishop of Canterbury, a link that goes back to the Reformation.",
 js:"A special lawyer who checks and stamps important papers.",
 jl:"A notary is a lawyer whose job is to check that important papers are real and signed by the right people. They add an official stamp. People often need this when sending papers to another country.",
 jx:"Grandpa visited a notary to stamp the papers for his house in Italy."},
{id:"l_statute",cat:"law",c:"United Kingdom",t:"Act of Parliament",
 s:"A law made by Parliament, also called a statute.",
 l:"A proposed law starts as a bill. In the UK it is debated and can be amended in both the House of Commons and the House of Lords, going through several stages called readings. When both Houses agree on the text, it receives Royal Assent and becomes an Act of Parliament. Acts can create new offences, rights, taxes or public bodies, and they can change or repeal older Acts. Courts interpret Acts but cannot strike them down, because Parliament is sovereign in the UK.",
 x:"The Equality Act of twenty ten brought many older anti-discrimination laws together into one statute.",
 f:"Some very old Acts of Parliament are still in force, including parts of statutes from the Middle Ages.",
 js:"A law made by the country's Parliament.",
 jl:"In the UK, laws are made in Parliament. First, people write down an idea for a law, called a bill. Members of Parliament discuss it and vote. If they agree, it becomes an Act of Parliament, a law everyone must follow.",
 jx:"Our class learned how a bill becomes an Act of Parliament."},
{id:"l_compensation",cat:"law",c:"England & Wales",t:"Damages",
 s:"Money that a court orders one party to pay another to make up for loss or harm caused.",
 l:"In civil cases, damages aim to put the injured person, as far as money can, in the position they would have been in if the wrong had not happened. They can cover things like repair costs, lost earnings and medical expenses, as well as pain and suffering. A claimant has to prove the loss and that it was caused by the other side. Many claims are settled out of court, where the parties agree an amount without a judgment. Damages are different from fines, which are paid to the state as punishment.",
 x:"The builder had to pay damages after his mistake flooded the neighbour's kitchen.",
 f:"In some rare cases a court can award nominal damages, a tiny sum that simply recognises that a person's rights were breached even though they lost little or nothing.",
 js:"Money someone must pay because they caused damage or harm.",
 jl:"If someone breaks something of yours by being careless, it's fair that they pay to fix it. In law, money paid to make up for harm is called damages. A judge can decide how much it should be.",
 jx:"The football broke our window, and the neighbour paid for the damage."},
{id:"l_terms_conditions",cat:"law",c:"United Kingdom",t:"Terms and conditions",
 s:"The written rules of a contract that you agree to when you buy something or sign up for a service.",
 l:"Terms and conditions, often shortened to T's and C's, set out things like prices, payment, delivery, cancellation, how your data is used and what happens if something goes wrong. By ticking a box or using a service, you usually agree to them, even if you did not read them. Under the Consumer Rights Act of twenty fifteen, unfair terms in consumer contracts are not binding on the consumer, and important terms must be written in plain, understandable language and made prominent.",
 x:"Before signing up for the gym, read the terms and conditions about cancelling.",
 f:"Some companies have hidden jokes in their terms and conditions to show how few people read them, offering a prize to anyone who spotted the sentence.",
 js:"The rules you agree to when you buy something or join an app.",
 jl:"When grown-ups sign up for an app or buy something online, there is often a long page of rules called terms and conditions. It explains what they can and can't do. Asking a grown-up to read it with you is a good idea.",
 jx:"Before downloading the game, Dad read the terms and conditions with me."},
{id:"l_en_barrister",cat:"law",only:1,c:"England & Wales",t:"Barrister",
 s:"A lawyer in England and Wales who specialises in representing clients in court and giving expert legal opinions.",
 l:"The legal profession in England and Wales is split into two main branches. Solicitors usually deal with clients directly and handle most legal work, while barristers specialise in advocacy in the higher courts and in detailed legal advice. Barristers are often self-employed and work together in offices called chambers. Experienced barristers can be appointed King's Counsel. In some courts barristers still wear wigs and gowns, though the rules on this have been relaxed in many hearings.",
 x:"The solicitor hired a barrister to argue the case in the Crown Court.",
 f:"To become a barrister, a person must join one of the four Inns of Court in London, Inner Temple, Middle Temple, Lincoln's Inn or Gray's Inn, and be called to the Bar.",
 js:"A lawyer in England who speaks for people in court, sometimes wearing a wig.",
 jl:"In England and Wales, some lawyers mainly help people in court. They are called barristers. They stand up and explain their side to the judge and jury. In some courts they still wear old-fashioned white wigs.",
 jx:"In the film, the barrister wore a curly wig and spoke to the jury."},
/* ---------- EVERYDAY LANGUAGE (+6) ---------- */
{id:"g_jargon",cat:"gen",t:"Jargon",
 s:"Special words used by a particular group or profession that outsiders often find hard to understand.",
 l:"Every field has its jargon: doctors talk about prognosis, gamers about respawning, and office workers about deliverables. Jargon can be useful, because it lets experts talk precisely and quickly. But when it is used with people who do not share it, it can confuse or exclude them, or make simple things sound complicated. Good communicators notice their own jargon and explain it or swap it for plain words when talking to a wider audience.",
 x:"The engineer's talk was so full of jargon that half the audience got lost.",
 f:"In medieval French and English, jargon originally meant the twittering or chattering of birds.",
 js:"Special words that only people in a certain job or hobby understand.",
 jl:"People who do the same job or hobby often use special words. Doctors, footballers and computer gamers all have their own. These words are called jargon. If you don't know them, it can sound like a secret language.",
 jx:"My brother uses so much gaming jargon that I can't understand him."},
{id:"g_etymology",cat:"gen",t:"Etymology",
 s:"The origin and history of a word, or the study of how words came to have their forms and meanings.",
 l:"Etymology traces words back through older forms and other languages. For example, it shows that many English words come from Latin, French, Old Norse or Greek, and that meanings shift over time. Etymologists use old texts and compare related languages, so their conclusions rest on evidence. Popular stories about word origins are often false; such invented explanations are called folk etymologies. Etymology is different from a definition: it explains where a word came from, not only what it means today.",
 x:"The etymology of 'salary' is often linked to the Latin word for salt.",
 f:"The word etymology itself comes from the Greek etymon, meaning the true sense of a word, and logia, meaning study.",
 js:"Where a word comes from and how it started.",
 jl:"Words have life stories. Some started in other languages long ago and changed over time. Etymology is finding out where a word came from. For example, the word dinosaur comes from Greek words meaning terrible lizard.",
 jx:"We looked up the etymology of 'breakfast': it means to break your night-time fast."},
{id:"g_tongue_twister",cat:"gen",t:"Tongue twister",
 s:"A phrase that is hard to say quickly and correctly because of similar repeated sounds.",
 l:"Tongue twisters crowd together sounds that are easy to mix up, such as s and sh, or p and b, so that saying them fast makes the mouth stumble. They are popular as games, but actors, singers, presenters and speech and language therapists also use them to warm up and practise clear speech. Every language has its own famous ones, and they often cannot be translated, because the difficulty lies in the sounds, not the meaning.",
 x:"'She sells seashells by the seashore' is a tongue twister most English children try.",
 f:"Because they depend on sounds, the hardest tongue twisters in one language can be easy for speakers of another language whose sounds differ.",
 js:"A silly sentence that is very hard to say fast.",
 jl:"A tongue twister is a sentence full of similar sounds, like Red lorry, yellow lorry. Try saying it quickly five times! Your tongue gets all mixed up. It's a fun game, and it also helps you speak clearly.",
 jx:"Try saying 'Peter Piper picked a peck of pickled peppers' three times fast!"},
{id:"g_loanword",cat:"gen",t:"Loanword",
 s:"A word taken from one language into another, often with little change.",
 l:"Languages constantly borrow words from each other through trade, travel, science, food and technology. English has loanwords from hundreds of languages, such as ballet from French, tsunami from Japanese, kindergarten from German and algebra from Arabic. Over time loanwords may change their spelling or pronunciation to fit the new language. Some people dislike borrowing, but it is one of the most normal ways languages grow.",
 x:"'Café' is a loanword from French, and some people still write it with the accent.",
 f:"The word robot entered English and many other languages from Czech, through Karel Čapek's play R.U.R. of nineteen twenty; it is based on a word for forced labour.",
 js:"A word that one language has borrowed from another.",
 jl:"Languages borrow words from each other, just like friends borrow pencils. English borrowed pizza from Italian, kangaroo from an Australian language, and karate from Japanese. These are called loanwords.",
 jx:"Did you know 'pyjamas' is a loanword that came into English from India?"},
{id:"g_mnemonic",cat:"gen",t:"Mnemonic",
 s:"A memory aid, such as a rhyme, phrase or pattern, that helps you remember information.",
 l:"Mnemonics link hard-to-remember facts to something easier: a rhyme, a silly sentence, a picture or a pattern. Acrostic sentences use the first letter of each word, such as Richard Of York Gave Battle In Vain for the colours of the rainbow. Rhymes help with spelling and dates, and the knuckle trick shows which months have thirty-one days. Mnemonics work because our brains find stories, images and rhythm more memorable than lists. The first m is silent.",
 x:"She used a mnemonic to remember the order of the planets.",
 f:"The word comes from Greek, and the ancient Greeks had a goddess of memory called Mnemosyne, said to be the mother of the Muses.",
 js:"A trick that helps you remember something.",
 jl:"A mnemonic is a memory trick. It can be a song, a rhyme or a silly sentence. For example, to remember the colours of the rainbow, some children learn Richard Of York Gave Battle In Vain: red, orange, yellow, green, blue, indigo, violet.",
 jx:"I made up a mnemonic to remember the order of the planets."},
{id:"g_en_rhyming_slang",cat:"gen",only:1,c:"United Kingdom",t:"Cockney rhyming slang",
 s:"A form of English slang from London's East End in which a word is replaced by a phrase that rhymes with it.",
 l:"In rhyming slang, a word is swapped for a phrase that rhymes with it, so stairs becomes apples and pears and phone becomes dog and bone. Often the rhyming part is then dropped, which makes it even harder for outsiders: use your loaf means use your head, from loaf of bread. It is linked with Cockneys, working-class Londoners from the East End, and was well established by the nineteenth century. A few expressions, like have a butcher's for have a look, are known across Britain.",
 x:"'I'm going up the apples to bed,' said Grandad in his best Cockney rhyming slang.",
 f:"By tradition, a true Cockney is someone born within earshot of the bells of St Mary-le-Bow church in the City of London.",
 js:"A funny London way of talking where you say a rhyme instead of the real word.",
 jl:"In parts of London, people used a secret-sounding way of talking. Instead of saying stairs, they said apples and pears, because pears rhymes with stairs. Instead of phone, they said dog and bone. It's a fun word puzzle.",
 jx:"Grandad said 'pass me the dog and bone', and I worked out he meant the phone."},
/* ---------- PSYCHOLOGY (+6) ---------- */
{id:"p_cognitive_load",cat:"psy",t:"Cognitive load",
 s:"The amount of mental effort being used in working memory at a given moment.",
 l:"Working memory can hold and juggle only a few things at once. When a task demands too much, for example new information, a confusing layout, background noise and a time limit all together, cognitive load becomes too high and learning or performance drops. Teachers and designers reduce it by breaking tasks into steps, giving clear examples, removing distractions and putting related information together. People with ADHD or anxiety, or anyone who is tired, may reach overload sooner, so reducing load helps everyone.",
 x:"Splitting the instructions into three short steps lowered the cognitive load for the class.",
 f:"Cognitive load theory was developed by the Australian educational psychologist John Sweller in the late nineteen eighties.",
 js:"How much thinking work your brain is doing at once.",
 jl:"Your brain can only hold a few things in mind at the same time. If too much happens at once, like a hard sum, noise and someone talking, it gets full and tired. Doing one small step at a time makes it easier.",
 jx:"I turned off the TV while doing homework so my brain wasn't so busy."},
{id:"p_pareidolia",cat:"psy",t:"Pareidolia",
 s:"The tendency to see meaningful patterns, especially faces, in random things such as clouds or electrical sockets.",
 l:"Pareidolia happens when the brain spots a familiar pattern where none was intended: a face in the front of a car, an animal in a cloud, a smiling mouth in a slice of toast. Our visual system is very quick to detect faces, which makes sense for social creatures; it is better to mistake a rock for a face than to miss a real one. Pareidolia is completely normal and common. It also works with sounds, when people hear words in random noise or in songs played backwards.",
 x:"Thanks to pareidolia, the two windows and the door made the house look surprised.",
 f:"The word comes from Greek: para, meaning beside or wrongly, and eidolon, meaning image or form.",
 js:"Seeing faces or shapes in things like clouds or plugs.",
 jl:"Have you ever seen a face in a cloud, or a plug socket that looks shocked? Our brains are so good at finding faces that they see them everywhere. This is called pareidolia, and everybody does it.",
 jx:"On our walk, we played a pareidolia game and found faces in trees and fences."},
{id:"p_bystander_effect",cat:"psy",t:"Bystander effect",
 s:"The finding that people are often less likely to help in an emergency when other people are present.",
 l:"When many people see someone in trouble, each one may assume that somebody else will act, or look at others' calm faces and decide that nothing serious is happening. Responsibility feels shared and therefore weaker. Knowing about the effect helps to overcome it: one useful tip is to address a specific person, for example 'you in the green jacket, please call for help'. Later studies show that in real, clearly dangerous situations, bystanders often do help.",
 x:"Remembering the bystander effect, she didn't wait for others and went straight to help.",
 f:"In a famous experiment from nineteen sixty-eight, Bibb Latané and John Darley filled a room with harmless smoke and found that people alone reported it much more often than people sitting with others who ignored it.",
 js:"When people in a crowd don't help because they think someone else will.",
 jl:"Sometimes, when lots of people see that someone needs help, everyone waits, thinking someone else will do it. Scientists call this the bystander effect. You can beat it: if you see someone hurt or upset, tell a grown-up straight away.",
 jx:"When a boy fell in the playground, Ella didn't wait for others, she ran to get the teacher."},
{id:"p_window_of_tolerance",cat:"psy",t:"Window of tolerance",
 s:"The zone of arousal in which a person can feel emotions and still think clearly and cope.",
 l:"Inside the window of tolerance, we can feel stressed, sad or excited and still think, learn and connect with others. Too much activation pushes us above the window into hyperarousal, such as panic, anger or racing thoughts; too little pushes us below into hypoarousal, such as numbness, shutting down or feeling far away. Stress, trauma, illness and lack of sleep can make the window narrower. Grounding, slow breathing, movement, rest and safe relationships can help people return to it and, over time, widen it.",
 x:"Her therapist used the window of tolerance to explain why she sometimes froze in arguments.",
 f:"The term was introduced by the American psychiatrist Daniel Siegel in his book The Developing Mind, published in nineteen ninety-nine.",
 js:"The calm-enough zone where you can think clearly even with big feelings.",
 jl:"Imagine a window. When you're inside it, you can have feelings and still think and talk. When feelings get too big, you might shout or panic; when you shut down, you might feel frozen. Deep breaths, a hug or a quiet corner help you get back inside the window.",
 jx:"When I feel too wobbly, I sit in the calm corner until I'm back in my window."},
{id:"p_halo_effect",cat:"psy",t:"Halo effect",
 s:"A bias in which one positive trait of a person or thing colours our judgement of their other traits.",
 l:"If someone is good-looking, confident or famous, we tend to assume they are also kind, clever or trustworthy, without real evidence. The same happens with products: a stylish brand may be seen as higher quality. The halo effect can influence job interviews, school marks, elections and shopping. The opposite, where one negative trait spoils our view of everything else, is sometimes called the horn effect. Using clear criteria and checking evidence helps to reduce it.",
 x:"Because of the halo effect, the interviewers rated the charming candidate highly on every skill.",
 f:"The psychologist Edward Thorndike described the effect in nineteen twenty after noticing that army officers rated soldiers as either good at everything or bad at everything.",
 js:"When liking one thing about someone makes you think everything about them is great.",
 jl:"Sometimes we meet someone who is very funny, and we quickly decide they must also be clever and kind and good at football. That's not always true! This mistake is called the halo effect, like an angel's shiny ring that makes everything look better.",
 jx:"The new boy was great at drawing, and because of the halo effect we thought he'd be great at maths too."},
{id:"p_en_keep_calm",cat:"psy",only:1,c:"United Kingdom",t:"Keep Calm and Carry On",
 s:"A British wartime slogan that became a modern symbol of staying steady under pressure.",
 l:"The poster with a crown and the words Keep Calm and Carry On was designed in nineteen thirty-nine to boost morale if Britain was attacked. Huge numbers were printed, but it was hardly ever displayed. Since its rediscovery it has been copied on mugs, bags and countless parodies. Psychologists note that calm coping is valuable, but so is expressing feelings and asking for help: carrying on does not have to mean keeping everything inside.",
 x:"Her desk has a Keep Calm and Carry On mug, but she still tells her friends when she's struggling.",
 f:"The forgotten poster became famous after a copy was found in two thousand at Barter Books, a second-hand bookshop in Alnwick, Northumberland.",
 js:"An old British poster that tells people to stay calm and keep going.",
 jl:"A long time ago, during a war, Britain printed posters saying Keep Calm and Carry On, to help people feel brave. Now you can see the words on mugs and T-shirts. Staying calm is good, but it's also fine to share when you feel worried.",
 jx:"We have a Keep Calm and Carry On fridge magnet in our kitchen."},
/* ---------- BUSINESS (+6) ---------- */
{id:"b_freelancer",cat:"biz",t:"Freelancer",
 s:"A self-employed person who works for several clients on separate jobs instead of being employed by one company.",
 l:"Freelancers, such as designers, translators, writers, photographers and programmers, find their own clients, set their prices and decide how much work to take on. This gives freedom and variety, but also uncertainty: income can change from month to month, and they usually have to handle their own taxes, pensions and holidays. Many freelancers use contracts, invoices and online platforms to find and manage work. Being a freelancer is different from working on a temporary contract for an agency.",
 x:"As a freelancer, he designs logos for small businesses in three countries.",
 f:"The word goes back to free lance, a term used by Walter Scott in his novel Ivanhoe of eighteen nineteen for a medieval soldier whose lance could be hired by anyone.",
 js:"Someone who works for different people on their own, not for one boss.",
 jl:"Most grown-ups work for one company. A freelancer works for themselves and does jobs for lots of different people. For example, a freelance artist might draw pictures for a book one month and for a website the next.",
 jx:"My aunt is a freelancer and draws pictures for children's books from her kitchen."},
{id:"b_logo",cat:"biz",t:"Logo",
 s:"A symbol, design or stylised name that identifies a company, product or organisation.",
 l:"A logo is the visual face of a brand. It may be a picture, such as an apple or a bird, a stylised word, or a combination. A good logo is simple, recognisable at small sizes, works in one colour and suits the organisation's personality. Companies protect their logos as trademarks and set rules for how they may be used. Logos are only one part of a brand, which also includes reputation, values and the experience customers have.",
 x:"The bakery's new logo is a smiling loaf of bread.",
 f:"The Nike swoosh was designed in nineteen seventy-one by Carolyn Davidson, a graphic design student, who was paid thirty-five dollars for it.",
 js:"A small picture or sign that shows which company something comes from.",
 jl:"A logo is a special picture or word design that a company uses so people recognise it. You can find logos on shoes, cereal boxes and shops. Some are so famous that you know the company just by seeing the picture.",
 jx:"For our school fair, we designed a logo with a sun and a book."},
{id:"b_agenda",cat:"biz",t:"Agenda",
 s:"A list of topics to be discussed or tasks to be done at a meeting, usually in order.",
 l:"An agenda tells participants what a meeting is for, what will be discussed, in what order and often for how long. Sending it in advance lets people prepare, bring documents and suggest additions. During the meeting, the chair uses it to keep discussion on track. Many agendas end with any other business, for points not planned in advance. In a wider sense, someone's agenda can mean their aims, and a hidden agenda is a secret aim.",
 x:"Please send me your points by Friday so I can add them to the agenda.",
 f:"Agenda is a Latin word meaning things that must be done.",
 js:"A list of things to talk about or do at a meeting.",
 jl:"When grown-ups have a meeting, they often write a list first: what they will talk about and in which order. This list is called an agenda. It helps everyone stay on topic and not forget anything.",
 jx:"The school council agenda had three points: the playground, the library and the bake sale."},
{id:"b_prototype",cat:"biz",t:"Prototype",
 s:"An early sample or model of a product, built to test an idea before making the final version.",
 l:"A prototype can be as simple as a paper sketch of an app, a cardboard model or a 3D-printed part, or as advanced as a working car that is not yet for sale. Its purpose is to learn quickly and cheaply: does it work, do people understand it, what breaks? Designers often build many prototypes, improving each one based on testing and feedback. A prototype is different from a minimum viable product, which is a simple version actually released to real customers.",
 x:"The team tested the cardboard prototype of the new chair with twenty users.",
 f:"The word comes from Greek: protos means first, and typos means a mark, impression or model.",
 js:"A first test model of something new.",
 jl:"Before inventors make something for real, they make a first try, called a prototype. It might be made of cardboard, tape or Lego. They test it, see what goes wrong, and make it better.",
 jx:"Our prototype for a bird feeder was made from a juice carton and string."},
{id:"b_barcode",cat:"biz",t:"Barcode",
 s:"A pattern of lines or squares that a machine can scan to identify a product quickly.",
 l:"A traditional barcode is a row of black bars and white spaces of different widths that encode a number. When a scanner reads it at the till, a computer looks up the product's name and price and updates the shop's stock records. This speeds up checkout and reduces errors. Two-dimensional codes such as QR codes store much more information in a grid of squares and can be scanned by phone cameras. The number under a product barcode identifies the product, not its price.",
 x:"The self-checkout beeped each time she scanned a barcode.",
 f:"The first product sold by scanning a standard barcode was a pack of chewing gum, in a supermarket in Ohio in nineteen seventy-four.",
 js:"The stripy lines on things you buy, which a machine reads at the till.",
 jl:"Look at a cereal box and you'll find black and white stripes with numbers. That's a barcode. At the shop, a machine beeps when it reads the stripes and instantly knows what the product is and how much it costs.",
 jx:"At the supermarket, I helped Mum scan the barcodes at the self-checkout."},
{id:"b_en_sole_trader",cat:"biz",only:1,c:"United Kingdom",t:"Sole trader",
 s:"In the UK, a self-employed person who runs their own business as an individual, not as a company.",
 l:"A sole trader owns the business personally, keeps all the profits after tax and makes all the decisions. It is the simplest way to start trading in the UK: there is no company to register, but sole traders must tell HM Revenue and Customs they are self-employed and usually file a Self Assessment tax return each year. The downside is unlimited liability: if the business owes money, the owner's personal belongings can be at risk. Many tradespeople, tutors and freelancers are sole traders.",
 x:"She started as a sole trader selling her pottery at markets before forming a limited company.",
 f:"Self Assessment tax returns filed online for the previous tax year are normally due by the thirty-first of January, which makes January a busy month for many sole traders.",
 js:"Someone in the UK who runs a small business all by themselves.",
 jl:"A sole trader is a person who runs their own little business on their own, like a window cleaner, a hairdresser or a piano teacher. They are their own boss and keep the money they earn, but they also have to sort out their own taxes.",
 jx:"Our piano teacher is a sole trader, and she makes her own lesson timetable."}
]);
